# tools/caravan_untyped_witness.rish -- the memory a dependent receives, and the one kernel refusal a real floor can answer.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `caravan/refusals.rye` stated the eleven answers a capability invocation may receive, mapped Caravan's
# four onto them, and named for each of the seven remaining which subsystem owes it. UNTYPED RETYPING owes
# `seL4_NotEnoughMemory`, and its debt reason names the shape of the answer outright: memory a dependent receives is
# carved from an untyped region with a real floor. `caravan/untyped.rye` is that region, and this is the last of the
# three subsystem debts -- after it the agenda narrows to edge checks.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN HERE, each on metal.
#
#   The owed refusal is answered by a REGION THAT KNOWS WHAT IT HAS SPENT. A carve the region cannot seat answers
#   `seL4_NotEnoughMemory` in the kernel's own symbol, imported from the module that binds the eleven against seL4's
#   BSD-2-Clause header at run time.
#
#   Every carved object stands on a boundary of ITS OWN SIZE, seL4's rule held as a postcondition, and THE CLIMB THAT
#   PUTS IT THERE IS SPENT -- a slot followed by a page costs the whole page boundary, and the region's yield says so.
#
#   A REFUSED CARVE SPENDS NOTHING. The watermark stands exactly where it stood, so a region cannot be eaten by
#   requests it declined.
#
#   THE CLAIM THIS MODULE REFUSED TO OVERSTATE. It would read well to say a raw remainder over-promises where alignment
#   bites. It is false for a naturally aligned power-of-two region, and the module says so and proves the true statement
#   instead: the aligned floor and the plain remainder AGREE, swept across every kind, every count from one to the
#   bound, and every reachable watermark over regions four to twenty bits wide at three origins apiece. The agreement is
#   bought by `open` refusing a misaligned origin -- which is why one of the three RED paths below removes exactly that
#   refusal.
#
#   THE SIZES ARE seL4's OWN, RE-READ FROM THE VENDORED HEADER on every run rather than recited -- `seL4_MinUntypedBits`,
#   `seL4_MaxUntypedBits`, `seL4_SlotBits`, `seL4_EndpointBits`, and `seL4_PageBits`. A vendored bump that moves one reds
#   this rung on the lap it lands.
#
#   THE CONFIGURED SIZES ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and the witness proves the absence is earned. In this very header
#   `seL4_TCBBits` and `seL4_NotificationBits` each stand behind an `#ifdef`, so a userland reciting one number for
#   either would be right on one build and wrong on another. The witness checks that both are still config-dependent
#   upstream, so the day seL4 states one unconditionally this rung is invited to carve it.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. No kernel booted and no untyped capability was retyped. This is policy on hosted ground: the
# floor is ours, written to the shape of the answer seL4 publishes, and it earns its behavioural proof the day a root
# task actually carves.
#
# Season: the Lindy-priority Microkernel Target double-seat (seated `20260817`, ordered ahead of Seasons A-H),
# Equinox 1 -- Caravan on the microkernel. Kin: tools/caravan_refusals_witness.rish (the vocabulary this pays into) -
# tools/caravan_ipc_buffer_witness.rish (the sibling debt, paid the lap before) -
# tools/caravan_derivation_witness.rish -
# external-research/20260821-043831_the-libsel4-reach-and-the-vocabulary-caravan-inherits.md
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/caravan_untyped_witness.rish

say "caravan-untyped: Language EN -- a region that knows how much of itself is spent is the only kind of region that can honestly say there is too little left."
say "caravan-untyped: Style Radiant -- four claims in order. Every object stands on a boundary of its own size. The climb that puts it there is spent. A refused carve spends nothing. And a naturally aligned region needs no second opinion."
say "caravan-untyped: Lens TAME -- the module's asserts are the specification, and all three RED paths are proven by planting them: a carve that never spends its climb, a refusal that eats the region, and an open that welcomes a misaligned origin."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "caravan/bin/untyped"
let errors = "vendor/sel4/libsel4/include/sel4/errors.h"
let arch = "vendor/sel4/libsel4/sel4_arch_include/riscv64/sel4/sel4_arch/constants.h"
let control = "tools/fixtures/caravan_untyped_control.sh"
let build_dir = "tools/.build/caravan_untyped"

let mk = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p caravan/bin ${build_dir}"]
assert mk.ok else "caravan-untyped: could not make the build directories"

# 0. PRECONDITION, an environment fact rather than a tree red: the gitlink must actually be checked out, since both the
#    vocabulary and every object size are read from seL4's own headers.
let checked_out = run ["sh" "-c" "test -f ${arch} && test -f ${errors} && echo yes || echo no"]
assert (trim checked_out.out) == "yes" else "caravan-untyped: vendor/sel4 is not checked out -- run `git submodule update --init --recursive` first; this is an environment precondition, not a tree red"

# 1. THE MODULE BUILDS AND PROVES ITSELF. Its asserts are the specification.
let build = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build caravan/untyped.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "caravan-untyped: the module did not build"

let test = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert test.ok else "caravan-untyped: the self-test exited non-zero"
assert test.out contains "GREEN" else "caravan-untyped: the self-test is not GREEN"
assert test.out contains "carved from a region with a real floor" else "caravan-untyped: the floor claim was not made"
assert test.out contains "after alignment answers seL4_NotEnoughMemory" else "caravan-untyped: the owed refusal is not seL4_NotEnoughMemory"
assert test.out contains "the padding that puts it there is spent" else "caravan-untyped: the spent-padding claim was not made"
assert test.out contains "a refused carve spends nothing" else "caravan-untyped: the untouched-on-refusal claim was not made"
assert test.out contains "the aligned floor and the plain remainder agree" else "caravan-untyped: the swept equivalence claim was not made"
assert test.out contains "between 4 and 38 bits and carves at most 64 objects" else "caravan-untyped: the module's own bounds moved without the witness moving with them"
assert test.out contains "refusing by named error" else "caravan-untyped: the bounds no longer refuse by named Rye error"

# 2. THE FLOOR IS REAL, and the worked carve shows both answers at once. An endpoint spends sixteen bytes; the page
#    that follows must begin on a page boundary, and in a page-wide region that boundary is the far end -- so four
#    thousand and eighty bytes stand unspent and the page is still honestly refused.
let table = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} table > ${build_dir}/table.txt 2>&1; echo done"]
assert (trim table.out) == "done" else "caravan-untyped: the worked carve did not print"

let printed = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${build_dir}/table.txt"]
assert printed.out contains "kind slot is 5 bits, 32 bytes" else "caravan-untyped: the slot size moved without the witness moving with it"
assert printed.out contains "kind endpoint is 4 bits, 16 bytes" else "caravan-untyped: the endpoint size moved without the witness moving with it"
assert printed.out contains "kind page is 12 bits, 4096 bytes" else "caravan-untyped: the page size moved without the witness moving with it"
assert printed.out contains "carving 1 endpoint answers seL4_NoError -- 16 bytes spent, 4080 free" else "caravan-untyped: the first carve did not spend exactly one endpoint"
assert printed.out contains "carving 1 page answers seL4_NotEnoughMemory -- 4080 bytes free" else "caravan-untyped: a page was not refused in a region that cannot seat one"
assert printed.out contains "next page boundary stands at 4096" else "caravan-untyped: the printed carve no longer names where the refused page would have to begin"

# 3. THE REFUSAL IS THE KERNEL'S OWN, printed through `refusals.rye` rather than spelled here, so the binding that
#    witness holds against seL4's BSD-2-Clause header carries through to this rung.
assert printed.out contains "seL4_NotEnoughMemory -- the untyped region holds too little to retype" else "caravan-untyped: the owed refusal did not carry its meaning"

let named = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'seL4_NotEnoughMemory' ${errors}"]
assert (trim named.out) == "1" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_NotEnoughMemory is no longer stated once in the vendored errors.h"

# 4. EVERY SIZE IS seL4's OWN, re-read from the vendored riscv64 header at run time rather than recited.
let min_bits = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'define seL4_MinUntypedBits     4' ${arch}"]
assert (trim min_bits.out) == "1" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_MinUntypedBits is no longer 4 in the vendored header -- min_untyped_bits must move with it"

let max_bits = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'define seL4_MaxUntypedBits     38' ${arch}"]
assert (trim max_bits.out) == "1" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_MaxUntypedBits is no longer 38 in the vendored header -- max_untyped_bits must move with it"

let slot_bits = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'define seL4_SlotBits           5' ${arch}"]
assert (trim slot_bits.out) == "1" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_SlotBits is no longer 5 in the vendored header -- the slot kind must move with it"

let endpoint_bits = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'define seL4_EndpointBits       4' ${arch}"]
assert (trim endpoint_bits.out) == "1" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_EndpointBits is no longer 4 in the vendored header -- the endpoint kind must move with it"

let page_bits = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'define seL4_PageBits          12' ${arch}"]
assert (trim page_bits.out) == "1" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_PageBits is no longer 12 in the vendored header -- the page kind must move with it"

let licence = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause' ${arch}"]
assert (trim licence.out) == "1" else "caravan-untyped: the riscv64 constants header is no longer tagged BSD-2-Clause -- the per-file sweep governs what we may read"

# 5. THE CONFIGURED SIZES ARE STILL CONFIGURED, so their absence from the roster stays earned rather than habitual.
#    Each appears twice in this header precisely because each stands behind an `#ifdef` with two answers.
let tcb_configured = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'define seL4_TCBBits' ${arch}"]
assert (trim tcb_configured.out) == "2" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_TCBBits is no longer stated two ways -- if the kernel now names one size, the roster is invited to carve a TCB"

let notification_configured = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'define seL4_NotificationBits' ${arch}"]
assert (trim notification_configured.out) == "2" else "caravan-untyped: seL4_NotificationBits is no longer stated two ways -- if the kernel now names one size, the roster is invited to carve a notification"

# 6. RED PATH ONE -- a carve that seats at the raw watermark never spends its climb, so a page lands off its own
#    boundary and the region's yield is a lie. Planted in a copy, built with the same compiler, swept after.
let padding = run ["sh" "-c" "sh ${control} ${zig} ${build_dir} padding 2>/dev/null"]
assert padding.ok else "caravan-untyped: the padding control did not run"
assert padding.out contains "planted=1" else "caravan-untyped: the unspent-climb copy was not planted -- the RED path would have proven nothing"
assert padding.out contains "verdict=refused" else "caravan-untyped: RED path one did not fire -- a carve seating at the raw watermark passed the self-test"

# 7. RED PATH TWO -- a refusal that eats the region turns a declined request into spent memory, and the second
#    movement reads the watermark after a refusal expecting it exactly where it stood.
let spend = run ["sh" "-c" "sh ${control} ${zig} ${build_dir} spend 2>/dev/null"]
assert spend.ok else "caravan-untyped: the spend control did not run"
assert spend.out contains "planted=1" else "caravan-untyped: the eating-refusal copy was not planted -- the RED path would have proven nothing"
assert spend.out contains "verdict=refused" else "caravan-untyped: RED path two did not fire -- a refusal that spent the whole region passed the self-test"

# 8. RED PATH THREE -- the sharpest. Natural alignment is what makes the aligned floor and the plain remainder agree,
#    so `open` refusing a misaligned origin is load-bearing for a claim proven three movements later by sweep. A copy
#    that welcomes a misaligned region must refuse itself.
let misalign = run ["sh" "-c" "sh ${control} ${zig} ${build_dir} misalign 2>/dev/null"]
assert misalign.ok else "caravan-untyped: the misalign control did not run"
assert misalign.out contains "planted=1" else "caravan-untyped: the misaligned-welcome copy was not planted -- the RED path would have proven nothing"
assert misalign.out contains "verdict=refused" else "caravan-untyped: RED path three did not fire -- a region opening off its own boundary passed the self-test"

say "caravan-untyped: a region carves objects onto boundaries of their own size, and the climb that puts them there is spent -- so a carve reporting room genuinely has it."
say "caravan-untyped: a carve the region cannot seat answers seL4_NotEnoughMemory, and the region stands exactly where it stood -- a floor refuses one request without closing the region."
say "caravan-untyped: the flattering claim was refused. A naturally aligned power-of-two region makes the aligned floor and the plain remainder agree, swept across every kind, count, and reachable watermark, and the refusal at open is what buys it."
say "caravan-untyped: every size is seL4's own, re-read from the vendored BSD-2-Clause riscv64 header on this run, with the two config-dependent sizes proven still config-dependent rather than quietly recited."
say "GREEN: Caravan carves memory with a real floor, and the last of the three subsystem debts is paid."
